WEBVTT

NOTE
Journey: Advocates — Week 5 Leader Video
Unit 2, Lesson 1: Worldview

1
00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:11.740
So, when I was 15, I remember going to my mom and sort of telling her, like, I'm having

2
00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:16.740
a hard time, like, seeing what's in front of me, I'm having a hard time seeing the board.

3
00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:21.100
I didn't think much of it, you know, because I didn't really just didn't think much of

4
00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:22.100
it.

5
00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:25.060
But I realized that I'm sitting in the back of class and where I like to sit.

6
00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:26.780
I couldn't see anything.

7
00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:31.140
And then it really got sort of serious when I started to drive and I realized

8
00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:35.500
that some signs that I think I might be, should be able to see or my friends

9
00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:40.460
have told me about that I'm not seeing, something's not right.

10
00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:44.420
And so, you know, I went to the doctors, went to the eye doctor, and they did all the little

11
00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:45.420
checks.

12
00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:48.980
They put the little thing on your face and they, you know, do all the dialing and you

13
00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:51.780
read the lines and the letters and all that kind of stuff, and they didn't think much

14
00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:53.280
of it.

15
00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:57.340
And they told me, you know, flip the lenses and which one is better and did the

16
00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:02.660
whole process of getting fitted for a pair of glasses.

17
00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:05.100
And I thought nothing of it.

18
00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:08.700
Until I walked in the day and picked them up.

19
00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:12.820
And I picked them up and I looked at them and for the first time I put glasses on and

20
00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:16.100
all of a sudden the world completely changed.

21
00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:17.100
Things were sharper.

22
00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:21.100
I could see things that I didn't even know existed from across the room.

23
00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:27.540
My entire perception, my entire reality really started to change because now I could actually

24
00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:30.380
see the way I was supposed to see.

25
00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:32.980
We're talking about world views.

26
00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:37.220
The most simplistic way to sort of see it and understand it is simply this.

27
00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:40.060
Is by looking at the world through a set of lenses.

28
00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:44.580
And how you look through those lenses sort of dictates how you see the world.

29
00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:48.100
World view, how I view the world, right?

30
00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:55.140
So, but more than that, world views and what you should start to now see through

31
00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:58.340
the world view questions that were introduced.

32
00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:01.140
But world views are foundational.

33
00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:04.500
They are life-shaping questions about reality.

34
00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:10.500
So it's not just the pair of glasses that I'm looking at through to see the world.

35
00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:17.180
It's literally how I'm interpreting it and how I'm living as a result of that view.

36
00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:21.460
So all those students should see very quickly through some of the activities in the Bible

37
00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:27.820
study that a biblical world view has a profound impact on how we live.

38
00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:34.420
So this week I want you to keep three really important things in mind as you work through

39
00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:35.420
this lesson.

40
00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:41.580
Now we've talked about things like what apologetics is and we've talked about what faith is.

41
00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:43.260
We've talked about what truth is.

42
00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:45.300
We've talked about logic.

43
00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:50.380
We've laid this foundation so that you can continue to move through this study.

44
00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:55.900
But now we need students to really understand what it means to see the world through a

45
00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:57.740
particular lens.

46
00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:02.900
But we don't just want them to see the world through the Christian lens.

47
00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:08.860
We want them to understand the world through a couple other popular ways of seeing, naturalism

48
00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:10.780
and pantheism.

49
00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:15.460
Then we're going to talk about what it looks like from a monotheistic world view.

50
00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:21.860
Because the way in which other people see the world is going to vastly, widely influence

51
00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:25.060
how your students start to see the world.

52
00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:29.580
So when people who see the world differently are involved in making movies and writing music

53
00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:34.020
and doing art and making TV shows and whatever else it is and writing and books and everything

54
00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:39.780
else, that world view is going to get communicated to your students.

55
00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:42.180
So they need to start to be able to see that.

56
00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:45.860
But before we can start into all of that, there's three really important things I

57
00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:49.340
want you to keep in mind because there's some things that are going to come up here

58
00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:51.740
that are going to be pretty impactful.

59
00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:57.460
Number one, don't assume your students have a biblical world view.

60
00:03:57.460 --> 00:04:03.300
Now, if your group has what most are going to call themselves Christians, I say your

61
00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:07.620
group is a typical youth group that has the majority of Christians.

62
00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:12.580
You might automatically just sort of assume that, hey, my students have a Christian

63
00:04:12.580 --> 00:04:17.740
world view or a biblical world view that may not necessarily be the case.

64
00:04:17.740 --> 00:04:23.100
You're going to discover that your students have some inconsistencies and some contradictions

65
00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:24.620
in their world view.

66
00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:29.740
In fact, world views are often, if you can imagine, going to a buffet restaurant.

67
00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:31.740
World views are often like that now.

68
00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:35.620
I mean, think about all the information that we have, think about all the, what's

69
00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:41.420
available to our students via the internet, that as soon as they want to know something,

70
00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:42.420
what do we ask?

71
00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:47.460
We ask Google, we ask Siri, we ask the questions over the internet to figure

72
00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:50.980
out the answers to our questions rather than asking, you know, we're going to a library

73
00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:52.940
and looking at a book and considering the source.

74
00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:58.380
We just ask and we look at the top five views on Google and we go with it.

75
00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:02.540
Our students have more information available to them than any other time in history.

76
00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:07.500
So what happens is students have what I call a buffet world view.

77
00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:10.900
You walk into a restaurant that's got a buffet and grab a plate and you just

78
00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:14.300
start taking food that looks good and you just start grabbing whatever that you

79
00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:18.860
want and then when you're done with that, you get rid of that plate, you go back to

80
00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:23.180
the buffet and you grab different stuff that looked good the next time or maybe

81
00:05:23.180 --> 00:05:26.540
you go back and grab some seconds of stuff that you really liked but you

82
00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:31.740
grab it all together and you just eat at a very low discounted price, right?

83
00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:37.500
So you may just automatically think that they have a biblical world view because

84
00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:42.740
somewhere along that buffet, there are pieces of Christianity, pieces of

85
00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:47.780
Scripture in their world view but then as you explore deeper, especially your

86
00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:52.940
small group leaders, as they explore deeper and get more into conversations and

87
00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:56.580
build deeper relationships with those students, they're going to discover that

88
00:05:56.580 --> 00:05:58.580
there's some significant inconsistencies.

89
00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:05.180
Now, don't panic, that's okay because as we move through this process, we'll

90
00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:09.060
hopefully iron out those inconsistencies and help them and guide them

91
00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:11.300
through a more consistent world view.

92
00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:15.300
That's what the actual world view questions are designed to do, right?

93
00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:21.140
By asking those four questions, it draws out what they think but it also reveals

94
00:06:21.140 --> 00:06:24.660
the flaws in thinking in a nice, gentle, respectful way.

95
00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:29.380
So make sure, encourage and hopefully you've created this environment

96
00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:33.140
already but encourage your students to answer honestly.

97
00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:37.460
We don't want the answers that they think we want to hear, we want their

98
00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:40.340
actual answers, whatever they may be.

99
00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:45.140
So don't freak out if you hear something that just totally you did not expect.

100
00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:51.300
Number two, world views have to be coherent and consistent.

101
00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:55.380
Even though the buffet sounds really good and it works for some, at least it

102
00:06:55.380 --> 00:07:00.740
seems like it works for some, it doesn't and over time that will cause problems.

103
00:07:00.740 --> 00:07:03.380
That's why you go back for seconds and you take different things because

104
00:07:03.380 --> 00:07:06.980
it's the search for trying to figure out the best possible combination that

105
00:07:06.980 --> 00:07:08.020
doesn't work.

106
00:07:08.020 --> 00:07:10.820
So you're going to, you know, throughout this process we're going to be helping

107
00:07:10.820 --> 00:07:16.660
students understand that this is what makes Christianity so compelling

108
00:07:16.660 --> 00:07:20.420
is that it's entirely coherent and it's entirely consistent.

109
00:07:21.140 --> 00:07:25.620
And finally, don't expect students to change their world view right away.

110
00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:29.780
Just because you see that they have a different world view or they have

111
00:07:29.780 --> 00:07:33.540
inconsistencies in their world view or there's some questions in their world view

112
00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:37.300
and you present, even at the end of this 32 weeks, if you present, you know,

113
00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:40.500
you're presenting the Christian world view, don't expect them that they're just

114
00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:43.940
going to go, oh, I'm going to put on a whole new pair of glasses and I'm going to

115
00:07:43.940 --> 00:07:45.380
see the world totally different.

116
00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:48.260
It doesn't work that way.

117
00:07:48.260 --> 00:07:50.260
In fact, it doesn't work that way at all.

118
00:07:50.260 --> 00:07:55.460
In fact, changing how we see the world is not as easy as just changing a

119
00:07:55.460 --> 00:07:57.300
prescription on a pair of glasses.

120
00:07:57.300 --> 00:08:00.660
It's a huge, huge adjustment.

121
00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:06.900
In fact, sometimes so big that it causes discomfort, it causes anxiety

122
00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:11.700
because people to change how you see the world is to really change almost your

123
00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:15.940
entire reality of how you understand things and how you answer big questions

124
00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:17.860
in life and how you interact with people.

125
00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:20.260
That's a lot of change.

126
00:08:20.260 --> 00:08:22.820
So be patient with your students.

127
00:08:22.820 --> 00:08:24.340
Walk alongside them.

128
00:08:24.340 --> 00:08:26.100
Help them figure it all out.

129
00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:31.620
All right, so over the last several weeks, we've challenged you with a lot of

130
00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:35.860
adjustments that you kind of need to make within the context of your group.

131
00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:39.460
We've talked about the idea of building relationships, being the most important

132
00:08:39.460 --> 00:08:39.940
thing.

133
00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:42.900
Not just the relationship between your students and Christ,

134
00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:45.860
but your relationships between you and your students.

135
00:08:45.860 --> 00:08:48.420
That there needs to be that sense of trust built.

136
00:08:48.420 --> 00:08:52.260
We've also talked about the idea of really just fostering a community

137
00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:54.340
where questions and doubt are welcomed.

138
00:08:54.340 --> 00:08:58.580
We've talked about this idea of worldviews and what this looks like and

139
00:08:58.580 --> 00:09:02.740
being patient with your students in determining what their worldview

140
00:09:02.740 --> 00:09:08.500
actually is and to not be surprised if somehow their worldview doesn't quite

141
00:09:08.500 --> 00:09:09.940
line up with what you thought.

142
00:09:09.940 --> 00:09:12.980
And we've even talked about sort of how to get rid of that barrier wall

143
00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:16.980
between what you're talking about in terms of objective truth and their

144
00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:20.260
view of truth in a very postmodern, relative to this day,

145
00:09:20.260 --> 00:09:26.100
post-truth kind of world that they're consistently exposed to in school

146
00:09:26.100 --> 00:09:28.020
and in a media and whatnot.

147
00:09:28.660 --> 00:09:29.620
That's a lot.

148
00:09:30.180 --> 00:09:33.220
That's a lot to take in over the course of four weeks.

149
00:09:33.780 --> 00:09:37.620
So now would be a really, really good time if you haven't done so already

150
00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:39.460
to check in with your small group leaders,

151
00:09:39.460 --> 00:09:40.980
check in with your other leaders,

152
00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:43.140
to see how these things are going,

153
00:09:43.140 --> 00:09:47.460
to make sure that the community is starting to coalesce together,

154
00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:50.340
that students feel like they can ask the questions.

155
00:09:50.340 --> 00:09:54.820
Check in with some of your key students who have the pulse of the youth group

156
00:09:54.820 --> 00:09:59.940
to sort of get a feel for where everybody is and are you accomplishing these goals.

157
00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:02.100
It might look to you like you are,

158
00:10:03.380 --> 00:10:08.260
but don't be surprised if there's a little bit of angst and a little bit of struggle.

159
00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:11.300
But don't panic either if it's not happening just yet.

160
00:10:11.300 --> 00:10:15.540
The important thing right now is to keep your hand on the pulse of your group

161
00:10:15.540 --> 00:10:18.100
so that you can navigate it as you go

162
00:10:18.100 --> 00:10:20.340
and you are increasing that level of trust

163
00:10:20.340 --> 00:10:23.780
and increasing the student's ability to ask the tough questions.

164
00:10:23.780 --> 00:10:30.660
You suddenly might fall back into old questions that were asked in different lessons

165
00:10:30.660 --> 00:10:35.140
because now students finally feel like they can ask that particular question

166
00:10:35.140 --> 00:10:38.660
or answer that particular question that we're asking of them.

167
00:10:38.660 --> 00:10:40.740
So just be ready for it,

168
00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:42.420
check in with your small group leaders,

169
00:10:42.420 --> 00:10:45.460
check in with a couple of students to figure out where everything is

170
00:10:45.460 --> 00:10:49.380
and then strategize as to how you might move forward.

171
00:10:49.380 --> 00:10:52.260
But remember when we're talking about worldviews,

172
00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:54.500
the three things you need to keep in mind

173
00:10:54.500 --> 00:10:58.100
is that your students may not have necessarily a biblical worldview

174
00:10:58.100 --> 00:10:59.220
that you think that they have,

175
00:10:59.780 --> 00:11:02.340
but their worldview needs to not be a buffet,

176
00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:04.660
but coherent and consistent.

177
00:11:04.660 --> 00:11:07.140
And don't expect them to change it right away.

178
00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:10.100
Be patient and I think that's probably the most important piece

179
00:11:10.100 --> 00:11:11.380
is be patient with them.

180
00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:12.900
Pray with them.

181
00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:17.220
Continue to walk alongside them to help them understand truth

182
00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:18.500
and how it fits in

183
00:11:18.500 --> 00:11:20.820
and how truth is going to change their reality

184
00:11:20.820 --> 00:11:22.580
and how understanding scripture

185
00:11:22.580 --> 00:11:25.140
and their understanding of truth

186
00:11:25.140 --> 00:11:31.220
is going to actually change their worldview slowly but also profoundly.

